# O1 Check — Performance & Complexity Rule

Before returning non-trivial TypeScript/JavaScript code, evaluate **production performance risk**
as if running `o1-check` against the change.

## Evaluate before returning code

- Cyclomatic complexity, nesting depth, and function length
- Nested loops over user or database data
- DB / ORM / network calls inside loops
- Unbounded queries (missing `limit` / `take` / pagination)
- Sequential awaits that could be batched with `Promise.all`
- O(n²) lookups where a `Map` / `Set` would be O(n)

## Avoid in production paths

- SQL or ORM queries inside loops
- `SELECT *` / `selectAll()` when only a few columns are needed
- `LIKE '%term%'` on large tables without a full-text strategy
- Offset pagination for large datasets when cursor pagination is viable
- Fetching from the database then filtering in memory when SQL can filter
- Deeply nested loops with `.includes()` / `.find()` on arrays

## Prefer

- Batch queries (`WHERE IN`, joins, single round-trip)
- Pagination with enforced max page size on list endpoints
- `Map` / `Set` pre-indexing before iteration
- `Promise.all` (with concurrency limits when needed) for independent async work
- Early returns to flatten nesting

## When returning non-trivial code

Include a **short complexity note** (e.g. “O(n) single query + O(n) in-memory map build”).

If estimated risk is **high** or **critical**, refactor before returning the code.

## Tooling

Run locally:

```bash
pnpm run o1-check -- apps/api/src
pnpm run o1-check -- apps/api/src --format json
pnpm run o1-check -- apps/api/src --fail-below 70
```

Package: `packages/o1-check` (`o1-check.config.example.json` for rule toggles).
